{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X1YpuWcocBT6WrdZeYwPMz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Chen Zaremba et al. \"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code\" arXiv 2107.03374 temperature pass@k Codex\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.03374\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2107.03374\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/88877344/Evaluating_Large_Language_Models_Trained_on_Code\"},{\"title\":\"huggingface.co\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/IntelligenzaArtificiale/code-generation/commit/1e7d2f05fdfd25eb4d318e6e1b5b601f2da3786a\"},{\"title\":\"summary-of-some-paper-in-cuda.readthedocs.io\",\"url\":\"https://summary-of-some-paper-in-cuda.readthedocs.io/en/latest/evaluation/EvaluatingLargeLanguageModelsTrainedonCode/\"},{\"title\":\"update table\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/codeparrot/code-generation-models/commit/19e72826f46f2f1d65ebca016496266a4961e973\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/94807952/Evaluating_Large_Language_Models_Trained_on_Code\"},{\"title\":\"hyper.ai\",\"url\":\"https://hyper.ai/en/papers/2107.03374\"},{\"title\":\"code eval\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/evaluate-metric/code_eval/blob/ae02279e972b7341201e2e9738a3478ec0f2e680/code_eval.py\"},{\"title\":\"jlking's picture\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/jlking/v2s/blob/main/evaluate/metrics/code_eval/README.md\"}]\n\nThe search turned up the arXiv paper and several secondary sources that describe its temperature and pass@k findings.\n\n- **Temperature vs. k:** For a 679M-parameter model, the paper reports an optimal temperature of 0.2 for pass@1 and 0.8 for pass@100 (for a 679M parameter model, the optimal temperature for pass@1 is $T^* = 0.2$ and the optimal temperature for pass@100 is $T^* = 0.8$). Higher temperatures help when many samples are drawn, which the authors attribute to greater sample diversity (Higher temperatures are better when the number of samples is large, likely due to the increased sample diversity.).\n- **Default sampling:** The main results use temperature 0.8 (All samples are generated with temperature 0.8.).\n- **Estimator:** The authors generate n ≥ k samples per task, using n=200 and k≤100 in this paper, and count the correct samples (we generate nk𝑛𝑘n\\geq k samples per task (in this paper, we use n=200𝑛200n=200 and k100𝑘100k\\leq 100), count the number of correct samples cn𝑐𝑛c\\leq n which pass unit tests). The HuggingFace code_eval metric implements this same estimator, with the paper cited as its source.\n- **Headline result:** 77.5% with 100 samples (Fig. 1; Table 1).\n- **Authors:** The first author is Mark Chen. Wojciech Zaremba is listed among the authors in the arXiv metadata, and the paper is cited as arXiv:2107.03374 (2021).\n\nSources: arxiv.org/pdf/2107.03374, ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2107.03374, and HuggingFace's code_eval metric page. Only the arXiv PDF and ar5iv are primary; the rest are secondary.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a867cf8-27b7-4a02-a79a-db20ed0dcd08","uuid":"ea8c1f50-d259-4d79-9ed5-1f7ff652c3a9","timestamp":"2026-10-30T22:14:35.494Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Chen Zaremba et al. \"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code\" arXiv 2107.03374 temperature pass@k Codex","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_018aEhsXL3W8KZwKUGXGZPkP","content":[{"title":"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.03374"},{"title":"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code","url":"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2107.03374"},{"title":"Evaluating La