{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RkkYdq3PvF4d15etAJXxYG","type":"tool_result","content":"I can't give the full abstract verbatim because the quoting limit is 125 characters. Here's a summary:\n\n- **Number of LLMs:** Nine popular LLMs, each paired with five prompt-engineering techniques.\n- **Temperature range:** Sampling temperature was raised from 0.0 to 1.6.\n- **Problem type:** Multiple-choice question-and-answer (MCQA) problems, randomly sampled from standard LLM benchmarks and covering various problem-solving tasks.\n\nThe authors report no statistically significant effect on performance for temperatures from 0.0 to 1.0."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a867cf8-27b7-4a02-a79a-db20ed0dcd08","uuid":"8812cc7e-ffdf-405a-b1f6-7a12f5dcfab9","timestamp":"2026-10-30T22:15:29.847Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":42440,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"I can't give the full abstract verbatim because the quoting limit is 125 characters. Here's a summary:\n\n- **Number of LLMs:** Nine popular LLMs, each paired with five prompt-engineering techniques.\n- **Temperature range:** Sampling temperature was raised from 0.0 to 1.6.\n- **Problem type:** Multiple-choice question-and-answer (MCQA) problems, randomly sampled from standard LLM benchmarks and covering various problem-solving tasks.\n\nThe authors report no statistically significant effect on performance for temperatures from 0.0 to 1.0.","durationMs":3666,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05201"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RkkYdq3PvF4d15etAJXxYG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7P2fuYTBnA8CkuoCPBraX","type":"tool_result","content":"I can't reproduce the full abstract verbatim, since quotes from source documents are limited to 125 characters. Here are the key findings, with short quotes and paraphrase.\n\n- **Claim tested:** The abstract examines the common claim that temperature is \"the creativity parameter\" of LLMs, given that higher temperature produces more diverse outputs.\n- **Method:** The authors ran an empirical analysis of LLM output at different temperature values, using a narrative generation task with a fixed context, model, and prompt. They scored outputs on four conditions for creativity: novelty, typicality, cohesion, and coherence.\n- **Novelty:** Temperature is \"weakly correlated with novelty.\" Overall, outputs became slightly more novel as temperature rose.\n- **Incoherence:** Temperature is \"moderately correlated with incoherence,\" which the authors describe as unsurprising.\n- **Cohesion and typicality:** There is \"no relationship with either cohesion or typicality.\"\n- **Conclusion:** The influence of temperature on creativity is \"far more nuanced and weak\" than the \"creativity parameter\" claim suggests. The authors also discuss ways to achieve more controlled LLM creativity instead of relying on chance through temperature changes.\n\nThe page text doesn't name the model(s) used. It only says the study used a \"predetermined fixed context, model and prompt.\" You would need the full paper (linked as PDF or HTML on the arXiv page) to find the specific model."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a867cf8-27b7-4a02-a79a-db20ed0dcd08","uuid":"4b18da65-524d-45c7-9915-7b8de26064eb","timestamp":"2026-10-30T22:15:31.861Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":42354,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"I can't reproduce the full abstract verbatim, since quotes from source documents are limited to 125 characters. Here are the key findings, with short quotes and paraphrase.\n\n- **Claim tested:** The abstract examines the common claim that temperature is \"the creativity parameter\" of LLMs, given that higher temperature produces more diverse outputs.\n- **Method:** The authors ran an empirical analysis of LLM output at different temperature values, using a narrative generation task with a fixed context, model, and prompt. They scored outputs on four conditions for creativity: novelty, typicality, cohesion, and coherence.\n- **Novelty:** Temperature is \"weakly correlated with novelty.\" Overall, outputs became slightly more novel as temperature rose.\n- **Incoherence:** Temperature is \"moderately correlated with incoherence,\" which the authors describe as unsurprising.\n- **Cohesion and typicality:** There is \"no relationship with either cohesion or typicality.\"\n- **Conclusion:** The influence of temperature on creativity is \"far more nuanced and weak\" than the \"creativity parameter\" claim suggests. The authors also discuss ways to achieve more controlled LLM creativity instead of relying on chance through temperature changes.\n\nThe page text doesn't name the model(s) used. It only says the study used a \"predetermined fixed context, model and prompt.\" You would need the full paper (linked as PDF or HTML on the arXiv page) to find the specific model.","durationMs":5682,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00492"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01J7P2fuYTBnA8CkuoCPBraX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TBkftNuhT98xotiW8FC9Up","type":"tool_result","content":"**Authors (in order):**\n\n1. Mark Chen\n2. Jerry Tworek\n3. Heewoo Jun\n4. Qiming Yuan\n5. Henrique Ponde de Oliveira Pinto\n6. Jared Kaplan\n7. Harri Edwards\n8. Yuri Burda\n9. Nicholas Joseph\n10. Greg Brockman\n11. Alex Ray\n12. Raul Puri\n13. Gretchen Krueger\n14. Michael Petrov\n15. Heidy Khlaaf\n16. Girish Sastry\n17. Pamela Mishkin\n18. Brooke Chan\n19. Scott Gray\n20. Nick Ryder\n21. Mikhail Pavlov\n22. Alethea Power\n23. Lukasz Kaiser\n24. Mohammad Bavarian\n25. Clemens Winter\n26. Philippe Tillet\n27. Felipe Petroski Such\n28. Dave Cummings\n29. Matthias Plappert\n30. Fotios Chantzis\n31. Elizabeth Barnes\n32. Ariel Herbert-Voss\n33. William Hebgen Guss\n34. Alex Nichol\n35. Alex Paino\n36. Nikolas Tezak\n37. Jie Tang\n38. Igor Babuschkin\n39. Suchir Balaji\n40. Shantanu Jain\n41. William Saunders\n42. Christopher Hesse\n43. Andrew N. Carr\n44. Jan Leike\n45. Josh Achiam\n46. Vedant Misra\n47. Evan Morikawa\n48. Alec Radford\n49. Matthew Knight\n50. Miles Brundage\n51. Mira Murati\n52. Katie Mayer\n53. Peter Welinder\n54. Bob McGrew\n55. Dario Amodei\n56. Sam McCandlish\n57. Ilya Sutskever\n58. Wojciech Zaremba\n\n**Abstract (paraphrased):**\n\nThe paper presents Codex, a GPT-based language model fine-tuned on public GitHub code, and evaluates its ability to write Python. A production version of Codex powers GitHub Copilot. On HumanEval, a benchmark the authors released for generating programs from docstrings, Codex solves 28.8% of problems, compared with 0% for GPT-3 and 11.4% for GPT-J. Repeatedly sampling the model proves an effective way to find working solutions to hard prompts, reaching 70.2% with 100 samples per problem. The authors also identify limitations, such as trouble with docstrings describing long chains of operations and with binding operations to variables. Finally, they discuss broader impacts of powerful code generation, including safety, security, and economic concerns."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a867cf8-27b7-4a02-a79a-db20ed0dcd08","uuid":"2ca3c973-1cbb-45ca-8ecb-b760ba6e7411","timestamp":"2026-10-30T22:15:33.691Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":51693,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Authors (in order):**\n\n1. Mark Chen\n2. Jerry Tworek\n3. Heewoo Jun\n4. Qiming Yuan\n5. Henrique Ponde de Oliveira Pinto\n6. Jared Kaplan\n7. Harri Edwards\n8. Yuri Burda\n9. Nicholas Joseph\n10. Greg Brockman\n11. Alex Ray\n12. Raul Puri\n13. Gretchen Krueger\n14. Michael Petrov\n15. Heidy Khlaaf\n16. Girish Sastry\n17. Pamela Mishkin\n18. Brooke Chan\n19. Scott Gray\n20. Nick Ryder\n21. Mikhail Pavlov\n22. Alethea Power\n23. Lukasz Kaiser\n24. Mohammad Bavarian\n25. Clemens Winter\n26. Philippe Tillet\n27. Felipe Petroski Such\n28. Dave Cummings\n29. Matthias Plappert\n30. Fotios Chantzis\n31. Elizabeth Barnes\n32. Ariel Herbert-Voss\n33. William Hebgen Guss\n34. Alex Nichol\n35. Alex Paino\n36. Nikolas Tezak\n37. Jie Tang\n38. Igor Babuschkin\n39. Suchir Balaji\n40. Shantanu Jain\n41. William Saunders\n42. Christopher Hesse\n43. Andrew N. Carr\n44. Jan Leike\n45. Josh Achiam\n46. Vedant Misra\n47. Evan Morikawa\n48. Alec Radford\n49. Matthew Knight\n50. Miles Brundage\n51. Mira Murati\n52. Katie Mayer\n53. Peter Welinder\n54. Bob McGrew\n55. Dario Amodei\n56. Sam McCandlish\n57. Ilya Sutskever\n58. Wojciech Zaremba\n\n**Abstract (paraphrased):**\n\nThe paper presents Codex, a GPT-based language model fine-tuned on public GitHub code, and evaluates its ability to write Python. A production version of Codex powers GitHub Copilot. On HumanEval, a benchmark the authors released for generating programs from docstrings, Codex solves 28.8% of problems, compared with 0% for GPT-3 and 11.4% for GPT-J. Repeatedly sampling the model proves an effective way to find working solutions to hard prompts, reaching 70.2% with 100 samples per problem. The authors also identify limitations, such as trouble with docstrings describing long chains of operations and with binding operations to variables. Finally, they discuss broader impacts of powerful code generation, including safety, security, and economic concerns.","durationMs":7511,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.03374"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TBkftNuhT98xotiW8FC9Up","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
