{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZCM7ZhepzS9R6wobwN9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PQR5GZLkVLYuHL9kUfkRBJ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/temperature_calibration_gaps_20261030_120000_ab12cd34/paper_draft\ncat > main.tex <<'E'\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[final]{neurips_2025}  % NEURIPS style (neurips_2025.sty is in paper_draft/)\n\\makeatletter\\providecommand{\\@trackname}{}\\makeatother  % [final] sets no track\n\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}  % REQUIRED: clickable links\n\\usepackage{booktabs}  % REQUIRED: professional tables\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usetikzlibrary{positioning,arrows.meta}\n\\usepackage{algorithm}\n\\usepackage[noend]{algpseudocode}  % before commands/general, which renews \\algorithmicindent\n\n% Import command files\n\\input{commands/math}\n\\input{commands/general}\n\\input{commands/macros}\n\n% Use this bibliography style:\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\title{No Universal Temperature: Quality Falls at Model-Specific Cliffs Across Tasks, Sizes, and Families}\n\n\\author{NeuriCo}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\input{sections/abstract}\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\input{sections/introduction}\n\\input{sections/related_work}\n\\input{sections/methodology}\n\\input{sections/results}\n\\input{sections/discussion}\n\\input{sections/conclusion}\n\n\\bibliography{references}\n\n\\end{document}\nE\npython3 - <<'E'\ns=open('commands/macros.tex').read()\nadd=r'''% Datasets\n\\newcommand{\\writingprompts}{\\textsc{WritingPrompts}\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\triviaqa}{\\textsc{TriviaQA}\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\humaneval}{\\textsc{HumanEval}\\xspace}\n\n% Metrics and derived quantities\n\\newcommand{\\passone}{pass@1\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\passfour}{pass@4\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\coverfour}{coverage@4\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\Tu}{\\ensuremath{T_u}\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\Tlo}{\\ensuremath{T_{\\mathrm{lo}}}\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\Tfifty}{\\ensuremath{T_{50}}\\xspace}\n\n'''\nm='%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\\n% METHOD NAMES'\nassert m in s\nopen('commands/macros.tex','w').write(s.replace(m,add+m,1))\nE\ncat > references.bib <<'E'\n@inproceedings{holtzman2020curious,\n  title={The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration},\n  author={Holtzman, Ari and Buys, Jan and Du, Li and Forbes, Maxwell and Choi, Yejin},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of ICLR},\n  year={2020}\n}\n\n@misc{chen2021codex,\n  title={Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code},\n  author={Chen, Mark and Tworek, Jerry and Jun, Heewoo and Yuan, Qiming and Pinto, Henrique Ponde de Oliveira and Kaplan, Jared and Edwards, Harri and Burda, Yuri and Joseph, Nicholas and Brockman, Greg and others},\n  howpublished={arXiv:2107.03374},\n  year={2021}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{renze2024temperature,\n  title={The Effect of Sampling Temperature on Problem Solving in Large Language Models},\n  author={Renze, Matthew and Guven, Erhan},\n  booktitle={Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2024},\n  pages={7346--7356},\n  year={2024}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{peeperkorn2024temperature,\n  title={Is Temperature the Creativity Parameter of Large Language Models?},\n  author={Peeperkorn, Max and Kouwenhoven, Tom and Brown, Dan and Jordanous, Anna},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of the 15th International Conference on Computational Creativity (ICCC)},\n  year={2024}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{joshi2017triviaqa,\n  title={{TriviaQA}: A Large Scale Distantly Supervised Challenge Dataset for Reading Comprehension},\n  author={Joshi, Mandar and Choi, Eunsol and Weld, Daniel S. and Zettlemoyer, Luke},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of ACL},\n  pages={1601--1611},\n  year={2017}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{fan2018hierarchical,\n  title={Hierarchical Neural Story Generation},\n  author={Fan, Angela and Lewis, Mike and Dauphin, Yann},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of ACL},\n  pages={889--898},\n  year={2018}\n}\n\n@article{holm1979simple,\n  title={A Simple Sequentially Rejective Multiple Test Procedure},\n  author={Holm, Sture},\n  journal={Scandinavian Journal of Statistics},\n  volume={6},\n  number={2},\n  pages={65--70},\n  year={1979}\n}\nE\ncat > figures/overview.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\\footnotesize, node distance=4mm and 7mm,\n  box/.style={draw=black!55, rounded corners=1.5pt, align=center, inner sep=3.5pt, minimum height=11mm, text width=27mm},\n  arr/.style={-{Latex[length=1.6mm]}, draw=black!60}]\n\\node[box] (m) {{\\bf 10 open-weight models}\\\\ Qwen, Llama, Mistral, Gemma; 0.5B--14B};\n\\node[box, right=of m] (t) {{\\bf 11 temperatures}\\\\ $T\\in[0,2]$, pure temperature sampling};\n\\node[box, right=of t] (d) {{\\bf 3 tasks}\\\\ stories, factual QA, code};\n\\node[box, right=of d] (s) {{\\bf Per-cell curves}\\\\ diversity and quality};\n\\node[box, below=of s, text width=27mm] (u) {{\\bf Per-model summaries}\\\\ \\Tu, \\Tfifty, curve shape};\n\\node[box, left=of u, text width=63mm] (c) {{\\bf Controls}: seed replicate (noise floor) and bf16 vs.\\ AWQ (precision)};\n\\draw[arr] (m) -- (t); \\draw[arr] (t) -- (d); \\draw[arr] (d) -- (s); \\draw[arr] (s) -- (u); \\draw[arr] (c) -- (u);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\nE\ncat > sections/abstract.tex <<'E'\nSoftmax temperature is the most widely exposed decoding setting, and practitioners choose it by folklore: 0.2 for code, 0.7 for chat, above 1 for creativity. We ask how output diversity and quality change with temperature on open-ended, factual, and coding tasks, and whether the useful range depends on model size and family. We sample 10 open-weight models from four families at 11 temperatures from 0 to 2.0 on stories, closed-book trivia, and executed code, for about 277,000 samples including two control runs. Quality does not decline gradually. It stays nearly flat and then falls at a point specific to each model: story coherence drops below 90\\% of peak at $T \\approx 0.84$ for Qwen2.5-0.5B and $T \\approx 1.31$ for Qwen2.5-14B. Code does not collapse just above 0.8 as folklore suggests: no model drops significantly from 0.6 to 0.8, and models of 3B and larger in the Qwen family, plus Gemma, stay within 90\\% of peak to $T \\approx 1.1$--$1.4$. Factual accuracy loses only 0.7--5.2 points at $T$ of 0.8--1.0, with no further coverage gain. Larger models tolerate higher temperature on all three tasks in the Qwen family, but only on QA for Llama, and Llama curves differ from the others by 4--7 times the seed noise floor. Temperatures below about 0.8 are safe on nearly every model tested but do little for diversity; above 1.0, the right setting has to be checked per model.\nE\ncat > sections/introduction.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nTemperature is the one decoding setting that nearly every large language model (LLM) interface exposes, and most users set it by habit. The common advice says to use a low value for code, about 0.7 for chat, and a value above 1 for creative work. Our main question is whether this advice matches what temperature actually does.\n\nThe answer matters because temperature trades off two things people want. Higher values make outputs more varied, which helps brainstorming, creative writing, and any workflow that samples several candidates. They also make outputs more likely to be wrong or broken. If diversity stops improving while quality keeps falling, a high setting buys nothing. If the safe range depends on the model, a single default is wrong for most deployments.\n\nExisting studies answer parts of this question. \\citet{renze2024temperature} found no significant effect of temperature between 0 and 1 on multiple-choice problem solving. \\citet{peeperkorn2024temperature} found only a weak link between temperature and story novelty for one fixed model. \\citet{chen2021codex} tuned temperature for code on one model family. However, each of these studies looks at a single kind of task, so none shows whether a limit found on one task carries over to another or how it moves with model size and family. On the other hand, the folklore makes exactly such claims. We state one version as a testable hypothesis: open-ended diversity plateaus above $T = 1.2$ while coherence drops 20--40\\%; factual accuracy degrades above 0.7 with no useful diversity gain; code has a narrow useful range of 0.2--0.6 with a sharp collapse above 0.8; smaller models have narrower useful ranges; and curve shapes differ across families at the same scale.\n\nWe test this hypothesis with one controlled grid (\\figref{fig:overview}). We run 10 open-weight models, two size ladders plus two models from other families, at 11 temperatures from 0 to 2.0 on stories, closed-book trivia, and executed code. All models use pure temperature sampling, so no other decoding setting hides the effect. For each cell we measure both diversity and quality, then derive each model's useful temperature range.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{0.95\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{figures/overview}}\n    \\caption{Study design. We cross 10 models, 11 temperatures, and 3 tasks, measure diversity and quality in every cell, and summarize each model by where its quality falls. Two control runs set the noise floors for comparing curves.}\n    \\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\nWe find that temperature fails abruptly and at a model-specific point. Story coherence falls below 90\\% of peak at $T \\approx 0.84$ for Qwen2.5-0.5B and at $T \\approx 1.31$ for Qwen2.5-14B. No fixed threshold describes all models. The code clause is largely wrong: no model drops significantly from $T = 0.6$ to $0.8$, and the largest relative drop from 0.6 to 1.0 is 28\\%. Greedy decoding is best or tied for best on \\passone for 5 of 10 models, while sampling four times at $T = 0.8$ adds 6--18 points over a single greedy attempt. Factual accuracy does fall above 0.7, but by only 0.7--5.2 points at $T$ of 0.8--1.0.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We measure diversity and quality on one grid across three tasks, 10 models, and temperatures up to 2.0, with seed and precision controls.\n    \\item We test five common claims about temperature and give each a clause-level verdict: one partly supported, one mostly supported with a small effect, one refuted as stated, and two supported with qualifications.\n    \\item We show that the useful range depends on both size and family, and that Llama models break earlier on stories than same-size models from other families.\n    \\item We show that much of the apparent diversity plateau is a measurement artifact: distinct-2 hits its ceiling and embedding diversity becomes unreliable on degenerate text.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\secref{sec:related} reviews prior work, \\secref{sec:method} describes our design, \\secref{sec:results} presents results for each claim, \\secref{sec:discussion} covers interpretation and limitations, and \\secref{sec:conclusion} concludes.\nE\ncat > sections/related_work.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\nThis project had no systematic literature search, so we position our work against four directly relevant studies rather than claim to cover the field.\n\n\\para{Temperature and degenerate text.}\n\\citet{holtzman2020curious} showed that high-randomness sampling produces incoherent text and proposed nucleus sampling to fix it. We do not compare samplers. Instead, we hold every other setting off and ask where pure temperature sampling breaks for each model.\n\n\\para{Temperature and accuracy.}\n\\citet{renze2024temperature} tested nine LLMs on multiple-choice problems at temperatures from 0 to 1.6 and found no significant effect between 0 and 1. Our models use open-ended trivia and code rather than multiple choice. With paired tests, we detect a small but significant accuracy loss at $T$ of 0.8--1.0 for 9 of 10 models, which agrees with theirs on the small size of the effect but not on its absence.\n\n\\para{Temperature and creativity.}\n\\citet{peeperkorn2024temperature} asked whether temperature is the ``creativity parameter'' of LLMs, using one fixed context, model, and prompt. They found that temperature is weakly correlated with novelty and moderately correlated with incoherence. Building on this, we track diversity and coherence together across 10 models, which shows how far the point of incoherence moves between models.\n\n\\para{Temperature and code.}\n\\citet{chen2021codex} reported that for a 679M-parameter Codex model the best temperature was 0.2 for pass@1 and 0.8 for pass@100, because higher temperatures give more diverse samples. Our \\passfour results follow the same pattern over a wider range of models. Unlike that study, we put code on the same grid as other tasks and test whether a narrow useful range holds across sizes and families.\nE\ncat > sections/methodology.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Methodology}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\para{Problem formulation.}\nGiven logits $z_i$ for the next token, temperature sampling draws token $i$ with probability\n\\begin{equation}\np_i(T) = \\frac{\\exp(z_i / T)}{\\sum_j \\exp(z_j / T)},\n\\end{equation}\nand $T = 0$ denotes greedy decoding. For a model $m$ and a quality curve $q_m(T)$, we define the useful range as the temperatures that keep quality near the model's own peak:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\mathcal{U}_m = \\{\\, T : q_m(T) \\ge 0.9 \\max_{T'} q_m(T') \\,\\},\n\\end{equation}\nwith linear interpolation between grid points. \\Tlo and \\Tu are its lower and upper bounds, and \\Tfifty is where quality falls to 50\\% of peak. We choose a relative threshold because models differ widely in absolute quality. This choice means that \\Tu says how far a model can go without losing much of what it has, not how good it is. For example, Qwen2.5-0.5B peaks at a coherent rate of only 0.85.\n\n\\para{Hypotheses.}\nWe split the claim into five sub-hypotheses. {\\bf H1 (open-ended):} diversity gain is much smaller above $T = 1.2$, while coherence at $T \\ge 1.4$ is 20--40\\% below peak. {\\bf H2 (factual QA):} accuracy above 0.7 falls, with little gain in coverage. {\\bf H3 (code):} the useful range is about 0.2--0.6, with a sharp collapse above 0.8. {\\bf H4 (size):} \\Tu grows with model size. {\\bf H5 (family):} curves differ across families at matched size.\n\n\\para{Models.}\n\\Tabref{tab:models} lists the models. No API key was available, so all subjects are open-weight checkpoints that we run locally with vLLM 0.32 on shared NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs. We did not test GPT, Claude, or Gemini. All runs use pure temperature sampling: \\texttt{top\\_p=1}, \\texttt{top\\_k} off, \\texttt{min\\_p=0}, \\texttt{repetition\\_penalty=1}, and \\texttt{generation\\_config=\"vllm\"} so that checkpoint defaults do not apply.\n\n\\input{tables/models}\n\n\\para{Design.}\nWe use temperatures $\\{0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6, 2.0\\}$, with 4 samples per (prompt, $T$) and 1 at the deterministic $T = 0$. This gives 23,124 samples per run and about 277,000 across 12 runs, and all runs completed all 6,204 (prompt, $T$) cells.\n\n\\para{Datasets.}\nWe use three tasks, each with one prompt template. {\\bf Stories:} 100 \\writingprompts \\citep{fan2018hierarchical} test prompts, up to 256 tokens. {\\bf Factual QA:} 300 closed-book \\triviaqa \\citep{joshi2017triviaqa} validation questions, up to 24 tokens. {\\bf Code:} all 164 \\humaneval \\citep{chen2021codex} problems, up to 448 tokens, run against the official tests in a resource-limited subprocess.\n\n\\para{Metrics.}\nFor stories, diversity is the mean pairwise cosine distance of \\texttt{all-mpnet-base-v2} embeddings across samples for a prompt (semantic diversity), plus distinct-2 over pooled samples. Quality comes from a Qwen3-8B judge that scores coherence from 1 to 9 as a probability-weighted digit, on 2 of 4 samples. We call the share of stories scoring at least 5 the coherent rate, and we also track lexical validity. All 25,200 judgments passed our logprob validity checks. For QA, we measure per-sample accuracy by normalized alias match, \\coverfour (any of 4 samples correct), and distinct answers. For code, we measure \\passone, \\passfour, syntax validity, and distinct programs by AST. Quality for \\Tu is the coherent rate for stories, accuracy for QA, and \\passone for code. Greedy decoding and the conventional $T = 0.7$ and $T = 1.0$ settings serve as baselines, and each model is compared with its own peak.\n\n\\para{Statistics.}\nWe use an item-level bootstrap with 2,000 resamples for 95\\% confidence intervals (CIs) and paired sign-flip permutation tests with 10,000 permutations and Holm correction \\citep{holm1979simple}. Holm correction runs over all 12 runs including controls. For size trends, we report Spearman correlations and bootstrap contrasts between the smallest and largest model of each ladder. The plateau test is the ratio of the diversity slope over $[1.2, 2.0]$ to the slope over $[0.2, 1.2]$. For family comparisons, the shape gap is the mean absolute difference between two curves, each normalized by its own maximum. We compare it with two measured floors: a seed replicate and a bf16-versus-AWQ control.\n\n\\para{Changes from the plan.}\nSeveral analysis choices came after seeing partial data, and we report them so readers can weigh the results. Shared-GPU memory moved the 14B and 3B models to AWQ, so precision co-varies with size in both ladders (bf16 at 1.5B and below, AWQ at 3B and above). We chose the coherent rate over the raw judge score for story \\Tu after seeing Qwen2.5-0.5B and Llama-1B results, because the judge rarely scores below 3. We edited the judge rubric once after inspecting five samples, before any scored run. We added the seed replicate, Holm correction, and ladder contrasts after the initial plan.\nE\ncat > tables/models.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Checkpoint} & \\textbf{Params} & \\textbf{Precision} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen2.5-0.5B, -1.5B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-\\{0.5,1.5\\}B-Instruct} & 0.5B, 1.5B & bf16 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-3B, -7B, -14B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-\\{3,7,14\\}B-Instruct-AWQ} & 3.1B, 7.6B, 14.8B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & \\texttt{unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct} & 1.2B & bf16 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & \\texttt{AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ} & 3.2B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & \\texttt{hugging-quants/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-AWQ-INT4} & 8.0B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Mistral-7B & \\texttt{solidrust/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3-AWQ} & 7.2B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Gemma-2-9B & \\texttt{hugging-quants/gemma-2-9b-it-AWQ-INT4} & 9.2B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Control: seed replicate & Qwen2.5-0.5B, different sampling seeds & 0.5B & bf16 \\\\\n        Control: precision & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct} & 7.6B & bf16 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Subject models. Each family is represented by one checkpoint per size, and the Llama ladder spans versions 3.2 and 3.1.}\n    \\label{tab:models}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/verdicts.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\small\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}p{5.2cm}p{2.5cm}p{6.6cm}@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Claim} & \\textbf{Verdict} & \\textbf{Evidence} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        H1: open-ended diversity plateaus above 1.2, coherence drops 20--40\\% & Partly supported & Measured diversity stops rising in 9/10 models but peaks at $T$ = 1.0--1.4 and then falls. Judge coherence falls 48--62\\% at $T = 1.4$ in 9/10 models. \\\\\n        H2: above 0.7, QA accuracy degrades with no diversity gain & Mostly supported, small effect & Accuracy at $T$ = 0.8--1.0 is lower in all 10 models, significantly in 9, by at most 5.2 points. \\coverfour stops improving above 0.7. \\\\\n        H3: code has useful range 0.2--0.6, collapse above 0.8 & Refuted as stated & No significant drop from 0.6 to 0.8. \\Tlo = 0 everywhere. Four models reach \\Tu of 1.07--1.42. \\\\\n        H4: smaller models have narrower useful ranges & Supported for Qwen, weak for Llama & Qwen: all three tasks. Llama: QA only. \\\\\n        H5: curve shape differs across families & Supported & Llama gaps are 4--7 times the seed floor. Qwen and Mistral are close to each other. \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Verdict on each clause of the hypothesis.}\n    \\label{tab:verdicts}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/stories.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Slope ratio}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Judge drop from peak}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Coherent rate}} \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7} \\cmidrule(lr){8-9}\n        \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Peak diversity $T$} & Semantic & Distinct-2 & $T$=1.2 & $T$=1.4 & $T$=2.0 & $T$=1.2 & $T$=1.4 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen2.5-0.5B & 1.2 & $-$1.00 & 0.03 & 44\\% & 50\\% & 50\\% & 0.03 & 0.01 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-1.5B & 1.2 & $-$0.38 & 0.07 & 53\\% & 60\\% & 61\\% & 0.07 & 0.00 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-3B & 1.4 & $-$0.45 & 0.23 & 17\\% & 48\\% & 62\\% & 0.86 & 0.22 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-7B & 1.4 & $-$0.47 & 0.38 & 17\\% & 53\\% & 64\\% & 0.88 & 0.20 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-14B & {\\bf 1.6} & {\\bf 0.98} & {\\bf 0.43} & {\\bf 3\\%} & {\\bf 19\\%} & 63\\% & {\\bf 1.00} & {\\bf 0.82} \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & 1.0 & $-$2.09 & 0.05 & 58\\% & 61\\% & 62\\% & 0.04 & 0.00 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & 1.0 & $-$1.41 & 0.12 & 55\\% & 62\\% & 62\\% & 0.10 & 0.00 \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & 1.0 & $-$1.65 & 0.09 & 57\\% & 62\\% & 63\\% & 0.07 & 0.00 \\\\\n        Mistral-7B & 1.4 & $-$0.81 & 0.42 & 15\\% & 49\\% & 63\\% & 0.995 & 0.13 \\\\\n        Gemma-2-9B & 1.4 & $-$1.02 & 0.26 & 10\\% & 53\\% & 64\\% & 0.95 & 0.09 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Open-ended generation. The slope ratio divides the diversity slope over $T \\in [1.2, 2.0]$ by the slope over $[0.2, 1.2]$. Most models lose about half their judge score by $T = 1.4$, and the coherent rate shows the loss is near total. Best value in each column is in \\textbf{bold}, except at $T = 2.0$, where all models sit at the judge's floor.}\n    \\label{tab:stories}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/qa.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Mean accuracy}} & & & & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{\\coverfour}} \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){8-9}\n        \\textbf{Model} & Greedy & $T \\le 0.7$ & $T$ 0.8--1.0 & \\textbf{Diff.\\ (pt)} & \\textbf{Holm $p$} & $T \\ge 1.2$ & $T$=0.7 & $T$=1.0 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen2.5-0.5B & .140 & .134 & .090 & $-$4.4 & $<$.001 & .030 & .217 & .167 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-1.5B & .370 & .360 & .330 & $-$2.9 & .001 & .198 & .467 & .430 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-3B & .410 & .393 & .367 & $-$2.6 & .001 & .234 & .447 & .463 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-7B & .553 & .545 & .522 & $-$2.2 & .001 & .348 & .593 & .603 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-14B & .627 & .621 & .615 & {\\bf $-$0.7} & .217 & .512 & .653 & .660 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & .317 & .314 & .262 & $-$5.2 & $<$.001 & .104 & .410 & .383 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & .553 & .545 & .497 & $-$4.8 & $<$.001 & .246 & .613 & .623 \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & .700 & .692 & .642 & $-$5.0 & $<$.001 & .346 & .763 & .760 \\\\\n        Mistral-7B & .723 & .713 & .693 & $-$2.0 & $<$.001 & {\\bf .548} & .753 & .757 \\\\\n        Gemma-2-9B & {\\bf .753} & {\\bf .744} & {\\bf .719} & $-$2.5 & .001 & .435 & {\\bf .810} & {\\bf .800} \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Factual QA. The difference compares $T$ of 0.8--1.0 with $T \\le 0.7$. Accuracy falls in every model and significantly in nine, but only by a few points. \\coverfour shows no consistent gain from 0.7 to 1.0. $p$-values use 10,000 permutations, so ``$<$.001'' is near the resolution limit. Best value in each column is in \\textbf{bold}.}\n    \\label{tab:qa}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/code.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & & & & & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textbf{\\passone}} & & \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){6-9}\n        \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Greedy} & \\textbf{Best $T$} & \\textbf{\\Tu (95\\% CI)} & \\textbf{\\Tfifty} & 0.6 & 0.8 & 1.0 & 1.2 & \\textbf{\\passfour at 0.8} & \\textbf{Holm $p$, 0.6 vs.\\ 1.0} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen2.5-0.5B & .360 & 0.4 & 0.58 (0.41--0.64) & 1.09 & .323 & .273 & .232 & .123 & .476 & $<$.001 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-1.5B & .585 & 0 & 0.81 (0.34--0.87) & 1.29 & .535 & .535 & .415 & .354 & .750 & $<$.001 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-3B & .640 & 0.2 & 1.07 (0.81--1.22) & 1.70 & .630 & .642 & .601 & .570 & .823 & .916 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-7B & .768 & 0.7 & {\\bf 1.42} (1.07--1.46) & 1.79 & .770 & .759 & .739 & .710 & .860 & .392 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-14B & {\\bf .841} & 0 & 1.15 (0.56--1.43) & {\\bf 1.82} & {\\bf .787} & {\\bf .793} & {\\bf .780} & {\\bf .750} & {\\bf .902} & .946 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & .341 & 0 & 0.44 (0.16--0.68) & 1.17 & .276 & .284 & .230 & .160 & .421 & .038 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & .549 & 0 & 0.72 (0.31--0.88) & 1.23 & .497 & .486 & .398 & .312 & .707 & $<$.001 \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & .628 & 0 & 0.69 (0.37--0.91) & 1.26 & .582 & .537 & .532 & .381 & .744 & .085 \\\\\n        Mistral-7B & .268 & 0.7 & 0.78 (0.14--1.05) & 1.39 & .264 & .238 & .241 & .191 & .421 & .946 \\\\\n        Gemma-2-9B & .610 & 0.4 & 1.34 (0.60--1.41) & 1.65 & .591 & .616 & .607 & .595 & .707 & .946 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Code generation. Best $T$ maximizes \\passone. Larger models hold their \\passone far past $T = 0.8$, while the four significant drops from 0.6 to 1.0 are all at 3B or below. \\Tu CIs are wide because curves are flat near the threshold. Best value in each column is in \\textbf{bold}.}\n    \\label{tab:code}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/size.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Qwen 0.5B$\\to$14B $\\Delta$\\Tu} & \\textbf{Llama 1B$\\to$8B $\\Delta$\\Tu} & \\textbf{Spearman $\\rho$, 10 models ($p$)} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Stories & +0.47 (0.43, 0.55) & $-$0.05 ($-$0.09, 0.00) & 0.61 (.060) \\\\\n        QA & +0.87 (0.76, 1.11) & +0.27 (0.04, 0.48) & 0.90 ($<$.001) \\\\\n        Code & +0.57 (0.00, 0.93) & +0.25 ($-$0.15, 0.59) & 0.64 (.048) \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\caption{Size effects on \\Tu, with 95\\% CIs for the ladder contrasts. The Qwen ladder gains range on every task, though the code CI barely clears zero. The Llama ladder gains on QA only.}\n    \\label{tab:size}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/family.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Pair (7--9B, all AWQ)} & \\textbf{Shape gap} & \\textbf{$T$s with CI excluding 0} & \\textbf{Peak $T$} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen 7B vs.\\ Llama 8B & {\\bf 0.220} & 9 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.0 \\\\\n        Llama 8B vs.\\ Mistral 7B & 0.205 & {\\bf 10} & 1.0 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        Llama 8B vs.\\ Gemma 9B & 0.118 & 8 & 1.0 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        Qwen 7B vs.\\ Gemma 9B & 0.104 & 8 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        Mistral 7B vs.\\ Gemma 9B & 0.098 & 9 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        Qwen 7B vs.\\ Mistral 7B & 0.046 & 2 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Floor: seed replicate & 0.031 & 0 & 1.2 vs.\\ 1.2 \\\\\n        Floor: bf16 vs.\\ AWQ & 0.026 & 1 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\caption{Family differences in semantic diversity curves, out of the 10 temperatures above 0. Llama pairs with Qwen and Mistral sit far above both floors, and Qwen and Mistral sit close to them. The largest values are in \\textbf{bold}.}\n    \\label{tab:family}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/failures.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}ccccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Code}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{QA}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Stories}} \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-6} \\cmidrule(lr){7-8}\n        \\textbf{$T$} & Passes & Syntax errors & Truncated & Correct & Rambling & Truncated & Non-ASCII \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        0.7 & 52\\% & 2\\% & 2\\% & 49\\% & 3\\% & 10\\% & 0.1\\% \\\\\n        1.0 & 48\\% & 4\\% & 2\\% & 47\\% & 2\\% & 14\\% & 0.5\\% \\\\\n        1.2 & 42\\% & 17\\% & 9\\% & 43\\% & 4\\% & 47\\% & 1.7\\% \\\\\n        1.4 & 32\\% & 45\\% & 35\\% & 37\\% & 12\\% & 70\\% & 5.5\\% \\\\\n        1.6 & 21\\% & 68\\% & 61\\% & 29\\% & 28\\% & 87\\% & 9.0\\% \\\\\n        2.0 & 5\\% & 93\\% & 89\\% & 11\\% & 58\\% & 92\\% & 11.4\\% \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\caption{Failure modes pooled over the 10 main models. Rambling means a wrong QA answer of more than 8 words. Between $T = 1.0$ and $1.4$, models stop ending their outputs and code stops parsing.}\n    \\label{tab:failures}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > sections/results.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\Tabref{tab:verdicts} summarizes our verdicts, and \\figref{fig:quality} shows the main pattern: quality holds near each model's peak and then falls, at a point that differs by model and task. \\Figref{fig:curves} shows all raw curves. Quality falls most abruptly on stories and more gradually on QA.\n\n\\input{tables/verdicts}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/quality_normalized.pdf}\n    \\caption{Quality relative to each model's own peak, with darker lines for larger models in a family. The dashed line marks 90\\%, which defines \\Tu. On stories, most models go from near their peak to near zero within one or two grid steps, and the break ranges from $T \\approx 0.8$ to 1.3 across models.}\n    \\label{fig:quality}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/curves_all.png}\n    \\caption{All metrics by temperature for all models and controls, with bands showing bootstrap 95\\% CIs. Vertical dashed lines mark $T = 0.7$ and $1.2$. Dotted gray lines are the control runs, which closely follow the matching main runs in shape.}\n    \\label{fig:curves}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{H1: open-ended generation}\n\\label{sec:h1}\n\n{\\bf Does diversity plateau above 1.2?} In a sense it does, but ``plateau'' is the wrong shape. Semantic diversity rises, peaks at $T$ of 1.0--1.4, and then falls in 9 of 10 models (\\tabref{tab:stories}). Qwen2.5-14B is the exception, with no slowdown between the two ranges (ratio 0.98, 95\\% CI 0.69--1.30).\n\n\\input{tables/stories}\n\nMost of this pattern comes from the measures, not the models. Distinct-2 flattens because it reaches its ceiling of 1.0. We read the fall in semantic diversity as an embedding artifact: degenerate text appears to embed similarly whatever it says, so a falling score does not mean outputs are less varied. We did not test this reading directly. Restricting to prompts where both judged samples are coherent supports it, because semantic diversity then rises at almost every temperature until too few coherent groups remain. For example, Gemma rises from 0.18 at $T = 0.2$ to 0.37 at $T = 1.4$. This is a selected subset, but it suggests that coherence loss, not saturation, ends useful diversity (\\figref{fig:frontier}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/story_frontier.pdf}\n    \\caption{Diversity against coherence as $T$ rises, with hollow markers at $T = 1.2$. Coherence stays flat while diversity rises, then drops steeply. \\figleft: semantic diversity turns back on itself past the cliff. \\figright: distinct-2 presses against its ceiling.}\n    \\label{fig:frontier}\n\\end{figure}\n\n{\\bf Does coherence drop 20--40\\%?} This prediction is met only in passing. At $T = 1.4$ Qwen2.5-14B falls 19\\%, close to the range, while the other nine models lose 48--62\\%. These figures understate the damage: the judge rarely scores below 3, so the raw scale cannot fall more than about 64\\%. The coherent rate shows it better, falling from about 1.0 to about 0 within one or two grid steps. For example, Mistral goes from 0.995 at $T = 1.2$ to 0.13 at $T = 1.4$.\n\n\\subsection{H2: factual QA}\n\\label{sec:h2}\n\n{\\bf Does accuracy degrade above 0.7?} Yes, but the loss is small (\\tabref{tab:qa}). Accuracy at $T$ of 0.8--1.0 is below that at $T \\le 0.7$ in all 10 models and significantly so in 9, by 2.0--5.2 points. Qwen2.5-14B loses 0.7 points, which is not significant ($p = .217$). The decline stays gradual up to about $T = 1.2$ and steepens beyond it.\n\n{\\bf Does the extra diversity help?} Below 0.7 it does: \\coverfour at $T = 0.7$ is 2.7--9.7 points above greedy accuracy. Above 0.7 it does not. Moving from 0.7 to 1.0 changes coverage by $-$5.0 to +1.7 points, $-$0.8 on average, even though distinct answers keep rising. The extra diversity is mostly more wrong answers.\n\n\\subsection{H3: code generation}\n\\label{sec:h3}\n\n{\\bf Is there a sharp collapse just above 0.8?} No (\\tabref{tab:code}). No model drops significantly from 0.6 to 0.8 after Holm correction. From 0.6 to 1.0 four models drop significantly, all at 3B or below, and the largest relative drop is 28\\% (Qwen2.5-0.5B), under the 30\\% we set as our example of a sharp drop in the plan.\n\n{\\bf Is the useful range 0.2--0.6?} The lower bound is not supported: $\\Tlo = 0$ for every model, and five models reach their best \\passone at greedy. The upper bound varies from 0.44 to 1.42, and Qwen2.5-3B, -7B, -14B and Gemma all stay within 90\\% of peak to at least 1.07.\n\n{\\bf Where does temperature pay off?} It pays off when several attempts can be tested. At $T = 0.8$, \\passfour exceeds greedy \\passone by 6--18 points in every model, led by Qwen2.5-3B at +18.3. Across models, the best temperature for \\passfour ranges from 0.4 to 1.4, and for five models it is 0.7 or 0.8.\n\n{\\bf Where is the real collapse?} It comes later and is syntactic (\\tabref{tab:failures}). Pooled across models, syntax errors go from 2\\% at $T = 0.8$ to 17\\% at 1.2, 45\\% at 1.4, and 93\\% at 2.0.\n\n\\input{tables/code}\n\n\\subsection{H4: model size}\n\\label{sec:h4}\n\nLarger models tolerate higher temperature in the Qwen family on all three tasks, though the code contrast only just clears zero and is not monotonic, since Qwen2.5-14B has a lower \\Tu than Qwen2.5-7B (\\tabref{tab:size}, \\figref{fig:ranges}). In the Llama family the trend holds on QA only. Story \\Tu is 1.01 at 1B, 1.00 at 3B, and 0.96 at 8B. Code \\Tfifty, which is less sensitive than \\Tu, also rises with size: 1.09 to 1.82 for Qwen and 1.17 to 1.26 for Llama. Spearman values within a ladder rest on 3--5 points, so we rely on the bootstrap contrasts instead.\n\n\\input{tables/size}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/useful_ranges.pdf}\n    \\caption{Upper bound of the useful range for each model, ordered by size, with 95\\% CIs. Story \\Tu shows Llama holding near 1.0 at every size while Qwen climbs with size. Code CIs are wide, so single-model code comparisons are weak.}\n    \\label{fig:ranges}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{H5: family differences at matched scale}\n\\label{sec:h5}\n\nCurve shapes differ across families, but not evenly (\\tabref{tab:family}, \\figref{fig:family}). Llama differs from the other three families by 4--7 times the seed floor, and it peaks at $T = 1.0$ rather than 1.4. The 1--1.5B and 3B Qwen-versus-Llama pairs show the same result, with gaps of 0.225 and 0.224. Gemma sits between the groups, and Qwen and Mistral are close to each other.\n\n\\input{tables/family}\n\nOn stories, family matters more than size in this sample. Llama-8B breaks at $\\Tu = 0.96$, close to Llama-1B at 1.01, while the other 7--9B models hold to 1.17--1.22. The evidence is weaker on the other two tasks: the seed floors for QA accuracy (0.061) and \\passone (0.051) are larger, and the largest 7--9B gaps reach only 0.115 and 0.213.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/family_matched_diversity.png}\n    \\caption{Diversity curves for the four 7--9B AWQ models. On stories, Llama peaks and falls earlier than the others. On code, Llama and Mistral become diverse at low temperature, while Qwen and Gemma rise slowly, so the family order is not the same on each task.}\n    \\label{fig:family}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Controls and failure modes}\n\\label{sec:controls}\n\n\\para{Seed.} Two sampling-seed runs of Qwen2.5-0.5B differ at no temperature on any metric.\n\n\\para{Precision.} Comparing Qwen2.5-7B in bf16 and AWQ, curve shapes sit at the seed floor (semantic diversity gap 0.026). Levels do differ: greedy \\passone is .829 versus .768, and greedy QA accuracy is .573 versus .553. AWQ therefore does not explain the shape differences above, but absolute levels should not be compared across precisions.\n\n\\para{Failure modes.} Above the cliff, models stop ending their outputs, drift into other languages, and emit code tokens in prose (\\tabref{tab:failures}). Story truncation at the token limit goes from 14\\% at $T = 1.0$ to 70\\% at $T = 1.4$.\nE\ncat > sections/discussion.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\para{What do the cliffs mean?}\nTemperature's useful range is set by where each model breaks, and fixed thresholds such as 0.7, 0.8, or 1.2 describe no single model well. The abrupt shape would fit a feedback process in which one random token that breaks the text makes further random tokens more likely, but we did not test this mechanism. We also cannot say why families differ. Logit scale and vocabulary are candidates, and we measured neither.\n\n\\para{What should practitioners do?}\nBelow $T \\approx 0.8$, temperature was safe on nearly every model we tested, but it is a weak diversity control. The common 0.7 default is therefore safe rather than optimal. Single-attempt code and QA do best at low $T$. If several attempts can be checked, moderate $T$ helps: \\passfour and \\coverfour both gain over greedy. Above $T \\approx 1.0$, the same setting can give intact output on one model and word salad on another of the same size, so the range has to be checked per model. At $T = 1.2$, Mistral-7B has a coherent rate of 0.995 and Llama-3.1-8B has 0.07.\n\n\\para{Limitations.}\nSeveral limits bound these conclusions.\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item {\\bf One judge with a compressed scale.} The Qwen3-8B judge rarely scores below 3 and shares a lineage with the Qwen subjects. An audit of 16 boundary stories agreed with the threshold on 12 (75\\%), with the judge slightly conservative. The audit was done by the research agent rather than by human raters, covers one model, and is small.\n    \\item {\\bf Embedding diversity misleads on degenerate text} (\\secref{sec:h1}).\n    \\item {\\bf Confounds.} Each family has one checkpoint per size, so family cannot be separated from checkpoint or quantizer differences, and precision co-varies with size. The Llama ladder also crosses versions 3.2 and 3.1.\n    \\item {\\bf Mistral's low code score.} Its \\passone of .268 is far below what its QA score suggests. Restoring prompt imports raised it only to .280, and most failures are real assertion errors, but the cause is unexplained. This score should not be taken as representative of Mistral-7B.\n    \\item {\\bf Scope.} We test pure temperature only, short stories, one prompt per task, and 4 samples per cell. $T = 0$ uses one sample, one seed replicate on one model is a thin noise floor, and vLLM batch composition can change outputs slightly. Results may not apply to hosted frontier models.\n    \\item {\\bf Resolution and thresholds.} Grid steps of 0.2 above $T = 0.8$ limit how precisely we can place a cliff, and \\Tu depends on the 90\\% threshold. Story \\Tu CIs are narrow partly because of interpolation.\n    \\item {\\bf Multiple comparisons} are corrected within tests but not across all hypotheses, and several analysis choices came after seeing partial data (\\secref{sec:method}).\n    \\item {\\bf Contamination.} All three datasets are public and may be in training data. This matters little for curve shapes and more for absolute levels.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\para{Broader implications.}\nInterfaces that offer a single temperature slider imply that a value means the same thing on every model, and our results show it does not. Benchmarks that compare models at one fixed temperature above 1 may partly measure where each model's cliff falls. Diversity metrics also need care, because a metric that falls on broken text can make a failing model look stable or a high setting look unproductive.\nE\ncat > sections/conclusion.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe measured diversity and quality on one grid of 10 open-weight models, 11 temperatures, and three tasks. Quality stays nearly flat and then falls at a model-specific point, with story \\Tu ranging from 0.84 to 1.31. Code has no sharp collapse just above 0.8, factual accuracy loses only a few points above 0.7 with no coverage gain, and the useful range grows with size in Qwen but mostly not in Llama. The main takeaway is that there is no universal temperature: the useful range is a property of the model, not of the setting.\n\nFuture work should measure logit scale and top-token margin to test whether they predict the cliff location, add more checkpoints per family to separate family from checkpoint, and run hosted frontier models through the same grid. Sampling at steps of 0.05 around each cliff would test how abrupt it really is, and a diversity measure that does not collapse on degenerate text, together with human coherence ratings, would remove the two largest measurement limits.\nE\npdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex >/dev/null; bibtex main | tail -3; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex >/dev/null; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex | grep -E \"^!|Overfull|undefined|Output written\" | head -30; wc -w sections/abstract.tex; grep -n \"figleft\\|figright\" commands/*.tex | head; grep -c . main.log; grep -E \"LaTeX Warning\" main.log | head","description":"Write all LaTeX sources and compile the paper"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1277,"cache_read_input_tokens":121715,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1277},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a867cf8-27b7-4a02-a79a-db20ed0dcd08","uuid":"389bca6b-5d01-4420-9447-b35e8c1cde0f","timestamp":"2026-10-30T22:20:31.482Z","request_id":"req_011CgZCM7MJWcoT1kqg1BDzT","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PQR5GZLkVLYuHL9kUfkRBJ":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/temperature_calibration_gaps_20261030_120000_ab12cd34/paper_draft\ncat > main.tex <<'E'\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[final]{neurips_2025}  % NEURIPS style (neurips_2025.sty is in paper_draft/)\n\\makeatletter\\providecommand{\\@trackname}{}\\makeatother  % [final] sets no track\n\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}  % REQUIRED: clickable links\n\\usepackage{booktabs}  % REQUIRED: professional tables\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usetikzlibrary{positioning,arrows.meta}\n\\usepackage{algorithm}\n\\usepackage[noend]{algpseudocode}  % before commands/general, which renews \\algorithmicindent\n\n% Import command files\n\\input{commands/math}\n\\input{commands/general}\n\\input{commands/macros}\n\n% Use this bibliography style:\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\title{No Universal Temperature: Quality Falls at Model-Specific Cliffs Across Tasks, Sizes, and Families}\n\n\\author{NeuriCo}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\input{sections/abstract}\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\input{sections/introduction}\n\\input{sections/related_work}\n\\input{sections/methodology}\n\\input{sections/results}\n\\input{sections/discussion}\n\\input{sections/conclusion}\n\n\\bibliography{references}\n\n\\end{document}\nE\npython3 - <<'E'\ns=open('commands/macros.tex').read()\nadd=r'''% Datasets\n\\newcommand{\\writingprompts}{\\textsc{WritingPrompts}\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\triviaqa}{\\textsc{TriviaQA}\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\humaneval}{\\textsc{HumanEval}\\xspace}\n\n% Metrics and derived quantities\n\\newcommand{\\passone}{pass@1\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\passfour}{pass@4\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\coverfour}{coverage@4\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\Tu}{\\ensuremath{T_u}\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\Tlo}{\\ensuremath{T_{\\mathrm{lo}}}\\xspace}\n\\newcommand{\\Tfifty}{\\ensuremath{T_{50}}\\xspace}\n\n'''\nm='%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\\n% METHOD NAMES'\nassert m in s\nopen('commands/macros.tex','w').write(s.replace(m,add+m,1))\nE\ncat > references.bib <<'E'\n@inproceedings{holtzman2020curious,\n  title={The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration},\n  author={Holtzman, Ari and Buys, Jan and Du, Li and Forbes, Maxwell and Choi, Yejin},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of ICLR},\n  year={2020}\n}\n\n@misc{chen2021codex,\n  title={Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code},\n  author={Chen, Mark and Tworek, Jerry and Jun, Heewoo and Yuan, Qiming and Pinto, Henrique Ponde de Oliveira and Kaplan, Jared and Edwards, Harri and Burda, Yuri and Joseph, Nicholas and Brockman, Greg and others},\n  howpublished={arXiv:2107.03374},\n  year={2021}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{renze2024temperature,\n  title={The Effect of Sampling Temperature on Problem Solving in Large Language Models},\n  author={Renze, Matthew and Guven, Erhan},\n  booktitle={Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2024},\n  pages={7346--7356},\n  year={2024}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{peeperkorn2024temperature,\n  title={Is Temperature the Creativity Parameter of Large Language Models?},\n  author={Peeperkorn, Max and Kouwenhoven, Tom and Brown, Dan and Jordanous, Anna},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of the 15th International Conference on Computational Creativity (ICCC)},\n  year={2024}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{joshi2017triviaqa,\n  title={{TriviaQA}: A Large Scale Distantly Supervised Challenge Dataset for Reading Comprehension},\n  author={Joshi, Mandar and Choi, Eunsol and Weld, Daniel S. and Zettlemoyer, Luke},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of ACL},\n  pages={1601--1611},\n  year={2017}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{fan2018hierarchical,\n  title={Hierarchical Neural Story Generation},\n  author={Fan, Angela and Lewis, Mike and Dauphin, Yann},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of ACL},\n  pages={889--898},\n  year={2018}\n}\n\n@article{holm1979simple,\n  title={A Simple Sequentially Rejective Multiple Test Procedure},\n  author={Holm, Sture},\n  journal={Scandinavian Journal of Statistics},\n  volume={6},\n  number={2},\n  pages={65--70},\n  year={1979}\n}\nE\ncat > figures/overview.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\\footnotesize, node distance=4mm and 7mm,\n  box/.style={draw=black!55, rounded corners=1.5pt, align=center, inner sep=3.5pt, minimum height=11mm, text width=27mm},\n  arr/.style={-{Latex[length=1.6mm]}, draw=black!60}]\n\\node[box] (m) {{\\bf 10 open-weight models}\\\\ Qwen, Llama, Mistral, Gemma; 0.5B--14B};\n\\node[box, right=of m] (t) {{\\bf 11 temperatures}\\\\ $T\\in[0,2]$, pure temperature sampling};\n\\node[box, right=of t] (d) {{\\bf 3 tasks}\\\\ stories, factual QA, code};\n\\node[box, right=of d] (s) {{\\bf Per-cell curves}\\\\ diversity and quality};\n\\node[box, below=of s, text width=27mm] (u) {{\\bf Per-model summaries}\\\\ \\Tu, \\Tfifty, curve shape};\n\\node[box, left=of u, text width=63mm] (c) {{\\bf Controls}: seed replicate (noise floor) and bf16 vs.\\ AWQ (precision)};\n\\draw[arr] (m) -- (t); \\draw[arr] (t) -- (d); \\draw[arr] (d) -- (s); \\draw[arr] (s) -- (u); \\draw[arr] (c) -- (u);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\nE\ncat > sections/abstract.tex <<'E'\nSoftmax temperature is the most widely exposed decoding setting, and practitioners choose it by folklore: 0.2 for code, 0.7 for chat, above 1 for creativity. We ask how output diversity and quality change with temperature on open-ended, factual, and coding tasks, and whether the useful range depends on model size and family. We sample 10 open-weight models from four families at 11 temperatures from 0 to 2.0 on stories, closed-book trivia, and executed code, for about 277,000 samples including two control runs. Quality does not decline gradually. It stays nearly flat and then falls at a point specific to each model: story coherence drops below 90\\% of peak at $T \\approx 0.84$ for Qwen2.5-0.5B and $T \\approx 1.31$ for Qwen2.5-14B. Code does not collapse just above 0.8 as folklore suggests: no model drops significantly from 0.6 to 0.8, and models of 3B and larger in the Qwen family, plus Gemma, stay within 90\\% of peak to $T \\approx 1.1$--$1.4$. Factual accuracy loses only 0.7--5.2 points at $T$ of 0.8--1.0, with no further coverage gain. Larger models tolerate higher temperature on all three tasks in the Qwen family, but only on QA for Llama, and Llama curves differ from the others by 4--7 times the seed noise floor. Temperatures below about 0.8 are safe on nearly every model tested but do little for diversity; above 1.0, the right setting has to be checked per model.\nE\ncat > sections/introduction.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nTemperature is the one decoding setting that nearly every large language model (LLM) interface exposes, and most users set it by habit. The common advice says to use a low value for code, about 0.7 for chat, and a value above 1 for creative work. Our main question is whether this advice matches what temperature actually does.\n\nThe answer matters because temperature trades off two things people want. Higher values make outputs more varied, which helps brainstorming, creative writing, and any workflow that samples several candidates. They also make outputs more likely to be wrong or broken. If diversity stops improving while quality keeps falling, a high setting buys nothing. If the safe range depends on the model, a single default is wrong for most deployments.\n\nExisting studies answer parts of this question. \\citet{renze2024temperature} found no significant effect of temperature between 0 and 1 on multiple-choice problem solving. \\citet{peeperkorn2024temperature} found only a weak link between temperature and story novelty for one fixed model. \\citet{chen2021codex} tuned temperature for code on one model family. However, each of these studies looks at a single kind of task, so none shows whether a limit found on one task carries over to another or how it moves with model size and family. On the other hand, the folklore makes exactly such claims. We state one version as a testable hypothesis: open-ended diversity plateaus above $T = 1.2$ while coherence drops 20--40\\%; factual accuracy degrades above 0.7 with no useful diversity gain; code has a narrow useful range of 0.2--0.6 with a sharp collapse above 0.8; smaller models have narrower useful ranges; and curve shapes differ across families at the same scale.\n\nWe test this hypothesis with one controlled grid (\\figref{fig:overview}). We run 10 open-weight models, two size ladders plus two models from other families, at 11 temperatures from 0 to 2.0 on stories, closed-book trivia, and executed code. All models use pure temperature sampling, so no other decoding setting hides the effect. For each cell we measure both diversity and quality, then derive each model's useful temperature range.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{0.95\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{figures/overview}}\n    \\caption{Study design. We cross 10 models, 11 temperatures, and 3 tasks, measure diversity and quality in every cell, and summarize each model by where its quality falls. Two control runs set the noise floors for comparing curves.}\n    \\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\nWe find that temperature fails abruptly and at a model-specific point. Story coherence falls below 90\\% of peak at $T \\approx 0.84$ for Qwen2.5-0.5B and at $T \\approx 1.31$ for Qwen2.5-14B. No fixed threshold describes all models. The code clause is largely wrong: no model drops significantly from $T = 0.6$ to $0.8$, and the largest relative drop from 0.6 to 1.0 is 28\\%. Greedy decoding is best or tied for best on \\passone for 5 of 10 models, while sampling four times at $T = 0.8$ adds 6--18 points over a single greedy attempt. Factual accuracy does fall above 0.7, but by only 0.7--5.2 points at $T$ of 0.8--1.0.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We measure diversity and quality on one grid across three tasks, 10 models, and temperatures up to 2.0, with seed and precision controls.\n    \\item We test five common claims about temperature and give each a clause-level verdict: one partly supported, one mostly supported with a small effect, one refuted as stated, and two supported with qualifications.\n    \\item We show that the useful range depends on both size and family, and that Llama models break earlier on stories than same-size models from other families.\n    \\item We show that much of the apparent diversity plateau is a measurement artifact: distinct-2 hits its ceiling and embedding diversity becomes unreliable on degenerate text.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\secref{sec:related} reviews prior work, \\secref{sec:method} describes our design, \\secref{sec:results} presents results for each claim, \\secref{sec:discussion} covers interpretation and limitations, and \\secref{sec:conclusion} concludes.\nE\ncat > sections/related_work.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\nThis project had no systematic literature search, so we position our work against four directly relevant studies rather than claim to cover the field.\n\n\\para{Temperature and degenerate text.}\n\\citet{holtzman2020curious} showed that high-randomness sampling produces incoherent text and proposed nucleus sampling to fix it. We do not compare samplers. Instead, we hold every other setting off and ask where pure temperature sampling breaks for each model.\n\n\\para{Temperature and accuracy.}\n\\citet{renze2024temperature} tested nine LLMs on multiple-choice problems at temperatures from 0 to 1.6 and found no significant effect between 0 and 1. Our models use open-ended trivia and code rather than multiple choice. With paired tests, we detect a small but significant accuracy loss at $T$ of 0.8--1.0 for 9 of 10 models, which agrees with theirs on the small size of the effect but not on its absence.\n\n\\para{Temperature and creativity.}\n\\citet{peeperkorn2024temperature} asked whether temperature is the ``creativity parameter'' of LLMs, using one fixed context, model, and prompt. They found that temperature is weakly correlated with novelty and moderately correlated with incoherence. Building on this, we track diversity and coherence together across 10 models, which shows how far the point of incoherence moves between models.\n\n\\para{Temperature and code.}\n\\citet{chen2021codex} reported that for a 679M-parameter Codex model the best temperature was 0.2 for pass@1 and 0.8 for pass@100, because higher temperatures give more diverse samples. Our \\passfour results follow the same pattern over a wider range of models. Unlike that study, we put code on the same grid as other tasks and test whether a narrow useful range holds across sizes and families.\nE\ncat > sections/methodology.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Methodology}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\para{Problem formulation.}\nGiven logits $z_i$ for the next token, temperature sampling draws token $i$ with probability\n\\begin{equation}\np_i(T) = \\frac{\\exp(z_i / T)}{\\sum_j \\exp(z_j / T)},\n\\end{equation}\nand $T = 0$ denotes greedy decoding. For a model $m$ and a quality curve $q_m(T)$, we define the useful range as the temperatures that keep quality near the model's own peak:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\mathcal{U}_m = \\{\\, T : q_m(T) \\ge 0.9 \\max_{T'} q_m(T') \\,\\},\n\\end{equation}\nwith linear interpolation between grid points. \\Tlo and \\Tu are its lower and upper bounds, and \\Tfifty is where quality falls to 50\\% of peak. We choose a relative threshold because models differ widely in absolute quality. This choice means that \\Tu says how far a model can go without losing much of what it has, not how good it is. For example, Qwen2.5-0.5B peaks at a coherent rate of only 0.85.\n\n\\para{Hypotheses.}\nWe split the claim into five sub-hypotheses. {\\bf H1 (open-ended):} diversity gain is much smaller above $T = 1.2$, while coherence at $T \\ge 1.4$ is 20--40\\% below peak. {\\bf H2 (factual QA):} accuracy above 0.7 falls, with little gain in coverage. {\\bf H3 (code):} the useful range is about 0.2--0.6, with a sharp collapse above 0.8. {\\bf H4 (size):} \\Tu grows with model size. {\\bf H5 (family):} curves differ across families at matched size.\n\n\\para{Models.}\n\\Tabref{tab:models} lists the models. No API key was available, so all subjects are open-weight checkpoints that we run locally with vLLM 0.32 on shared NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs. We did not test GPT, Claude, or Gemini. All runs use pure temperature sampling: \\texttt{top\\_p=1}, \\texttt{top\\_k} off, \\texttt{min\\_p=0}, \\texttt{repetition\\_penalty=1}, and \\texttt{generation\\_config=\"vllm\"} so that checkpoint defaults do not apply.\n\n\\input{tables/models}\n\n\\para{Design.}\nWe use temperatures $\\{0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6, 2.0\\}$, with 4 samples per (prompt, $T$) and 1 at the deterministic $T = 0$. This gives 23,124 samples per run and about 277,000 across 12 runs, and all runs completed all 6,204 (prompt, $T$) cells.\n\n\\para{Datasets.}\nWe use three tasks, each with one prompt template. {\\bf Stories:} 100 \\writingprompts \\citep{fan2018hierarchical} test prompts, up to 256 tokens. {\\bf Factual QA:} 300 closed-book \\triviaqa \\citep{joshi2017triviaqa} validation questions, up to 24 tokens. {\\bf Code:} all 164 \\humaneval \\citep{chen2021codex} problems, up to 448 tokens, run against the official tests in a resource-limited subprocess.\n\n\\para{Metrics.}\nFor stories, diversity is the mean pairwise cosine distance of \\texttt{all-mpnet-base-v2} embeddings across samples for a prompt (semantic diversity), plus distinct-2 over pooled samples. Quality comes from a Qwen3-8B judge that scores coherence from 1 to 9 as a probability-weighted digit, on 2 of 4 samples. We call the share of stories scoring at least 5 the coherent rate, and we also track lexical validity. All 25,200 judgments passed our logprob validity checks. For QA, we measure per-sample accuracy by normalized alias match, \\coverfour (any of 4 samples correct), and distinct answers. For code, we measure \\passone, \\passfour, syntax validity, and distinct programs by AST. Quality for \\Tu is the coherent rate for stories, accuracy for QA, and \\passone for code. Greedy decoding and the conventional $T = 0.7$ and $T = 1.0$ settings serve as baselines, and each model is compared with its own peak.\n\n\\para{Statistics.}\nWe use an item-level bootstrap with 2,000 resamples for 95\\% confidence intervals (CIs) and paired sign-flip permutation tests with 10,000 permutations and Holm correction \\citep{holm1979simple}. Holm correction runs over all 12 runs including controls. For size trends, we report Spearman correlations and bootstrap contrasts between the smallest and largest model of each ladder. The plateau test is the ratio of the diversity slope over $[1.2, 2.0]$ to the slope over $[0.2, 1.2]$. For family comparisons, the shape gap is the mean absolute difference between two curves, each normalized by its own maximum. We compare it with two measured floors: a seed replicate and a bf16-versus-AWQ control.\n\n\\para{Changes from the plan.}\nSeveral analysis choices came after seeing partial data, and we report them so readers can weigh the results. Shared-GPU memory moved the 14B and 3B models to AWQ, so precision co-varies with size in both ladders (bf16 at 1.5B and below, AWQ at 3B and above). We chose the coherent rate over the raw judge score for story \\Tu after seeing Qwen2.5-0.5B and Llama-1B results, because the judge rarely scores below 3. We edited the judge rubric once after inspecting five samples, before any scored run. We added the seed replicate, Holm correction, and ladder contrasts after the initial plan.\nE\ncat > tables/models.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Checkpoint} & \\textbf{Params} & \\textbf{Precision} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen2.5-0.5B, -1.5B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-\\{0.5,1.5\\}B-Instruct} & 0.5B, 1.5B & bf16 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-3B, -7B, -14B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-\\{3,7,14\\}B-Instruct-AWQ} & 3.1B, 7.6B, 14.8B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & \\texttt{unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct} & 1.2B & bf16 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & \\texttt{AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ} & 3.2B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & \\texttt{hugging-quants/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-AWQ-INT4} & 8.0B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Mistral-7B & \\texttt{solidrust/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3-AWQ} & 7.2B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Gemma-2-9B & \\texttt{hugging-quants/gemma-2-9b-it-AWQ-INT4} & 9.2B & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Control: seed replicate & Qwen2.5-0.5B, different sampling seeds & 0.5B & bf16 \\\\\n        Control: precision & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct} & 7.6B & bf16 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Subject models. Each family is represented by one checkpoint per size, and the Llama ladder spans versions 3.2 and 3.1.}\n    \\label{tab:models}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/verdicts.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\small\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}p{5.2cm}p{2.5cm}p{6.6cm}@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Claim} & \\textbf{Verdict} & \\textbf{Evidence} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        H1: open-ended diversity plateaus above 1.2, coherence drops 20--40\\% & Partly supported & Measured diversity stops rising in 9/10 models but peaks at $T$ = 1.0--1.4 and then falls. Judge coherence falls 48--62\\% at $T = 1.4$ in 9/10 models. \\\\\n        H2: above 0.7, QA accuracy degrades with no diversity gain & Mostly supported, small effect & Accuracy at $T$ = 0.8--1.0 is lower in all 10 models, significantly in 9, by at most 5.2 points. \\coverfour stops improving above 0.7. \\\\\n        H3: code has useful range 0.2--0.6, collapse above 0.8 & Refuted as stated & No significant drop from 0.6 to 0.8. \\Tlo = 0 everywhere. Four models reach \\Tu of 1.07--1.42. \\\\\n        H4: smaller models have narrower useful ranges & Supported for Qwen, weak for Llama & Qwen: all three tasks. Llama: QA only. \\\\\n        H5: curve shape differs across families & Supported & Llama gaps are 4--7 times the seed floor. Qwen and Mistral are close to each other. \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Verdict on each clause of the hypothesis.}\n    \\label{tab:verdicts}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/stories.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Slope ratio}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Judge drop from peak}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Coherent rate}} \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7} \\cmidrule(lr){8-9}\n        \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Peak diversity $T$} & Semantic & Distinct-2 & $T$=1.2 & $T$=1.4 & $T$=2.0 & $T$=1.2 & $T$=1.4 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen2.5-0.5B & 1.2 & $-$1.00 & 0.03 & 44\\% & 50\\% & 50\\% & 0.03 & 0.01 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-1.5B & 1.2 & $-$0.38 & 0.07 & 53\\% & 60\\% & 61\\% & 0.07 & 0.00 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-3B & 1.4 & $-$0.45 & 0.23 & 17\\% & 48\\% & 62\\% & 0.86 & 0.22 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-7B & 1.4 & $-$0.47 & 0.38 & 17\\% & 53\\% & 64\\% & 0.88 & 0.20 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-14B & {\\bf 1.6} & {\\bf 0.98} & {\\bf 0.43} & {\\bf 3\\%} & {\\bf 19\\%} & 63\\% & {\\bf 1.00} & {\\bf 0.82} \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & 1.0 & $-$2.09 & 0.05 & 58\\% & 61\\% & 62\\% & 0.04 & 0.00 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & 1.0 & $-$1.41 & 0.12 & 55\\% & 62\\% & 62\\% & 0.10 & 0.00 \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & 1.0 & $-$1.65 & 0.09 & 57\\% & 62\\% & 63\\% & 0.07 & 0.00 \\\\\n        Mistral-7B & 1.4 & $-$0.81 & 0.42 & 15\\% & 49\\% & 63\\% & 0.995 & 0.13 \\\\\n        Gemma-2-9B & 1.4 & $-$1.02 & 0.26 & 10\\% & 53\\% & 64\\% & 0.95 & 0.09 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Open-ended generation. The slope ratio divides the diversity slope over $T \\in [1.2, 2.0]$ by the slope over $[0.2, 1.2]$. Most models lose about half their judge score by $T = 1.4$, and the coherent rate shows the loss is near total. Best value in each column is in \\textbf{bold}, except at $T = 2.0$, where all models sit at the judge's floor.}\n    \\label{tab:stories}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/qa.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Mean accuracy}} & & & & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{\\coverfour}} \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){8-9}\n        \\textbf{Model} & Greedy & $T \\le 0.7$ & $T$ 0.8--1.0 & \\textbf{Diff.\\ (pt)} & \\textbf{Holm $p$} & $T \\ge 1.2$ & $T$=0.7 & $T$=1.0 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen2.5-0.5B & .140 & .134 & .090 & $-$4.4 & $<$.001 & .030 & .217 & .167 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-1.5B & .370 & .360 & .330 & $-$2.9 & .001 & .198 & .467 & .430 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-3B & .410 & .393 & .367 & $-$2.6 & .001 & .234 & .447 & .463 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-7B & .553 & .545 & .522 & $-$2.2 & .001 & .348 & .593 & .603 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-14B & .627 & .621 & .615 & {\\bf $-$0.7} & .217 & .512 & .653 & .660 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & .317 & .314 & .262 & $-$5.2 & $<$.001 & .104 & .410 & .383 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & .553 & .545 & .497 & $-$4.8 & $<$.001 & .246 & .613 & .623 \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & .700 & .692 & .642 & $-$5.0 & $<$.001 & .346 & .763 & .760 \\\\\n        Mistral-7B & .723 & .713 & .693 & $-$2.0 & $<$.001 & {\\bf .548} & .753 & .757 \\\\\n        Gemma-2-9B & {\\bf .753} & {\\bf .744} & {\\bf .719} & $-$2.5 & .001 & .435 & {\\bf .810} & {\\bf .800} \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Factual QA. The difference compares $T$ of 0.8--1.0 with $T \\le 0.7$. Accuracy falls in every model and significantly in nine, but only by a few points. \\coverfour shows no consistent gain from 0.7 to 1.0. $p$-values use 10,000 permutations, so ``$<$.001'' is near the resolution limit. Best value in each column is in \\textbf{bold}.}\n    \\label{tab:qa}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/code.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & & & & & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textbf{\\passone}} & & \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){6-9}\n        \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Greedy} & \\textbf{Best $T$} & \\textbf{\\Tu (95\\% CI)} & \\textbf{\\Tfifty} & 0.6 & 0.8 & 1.0 & 1.2 & \\textbf{\\passfour at 0.8} & \\textbf{Holm $p$, 0.6 vs.\\ 1.0} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen2.5-0.5B & .360 & 0.4 & 0.58 (0.41--0.64) & 1.09 & .323 & .273 & .232 & .123 & .476 & $<$.001 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-1.5B & .585 & 0 & 0.81 (0.34--0.87) & 1.29 & .535 & .535 & .415 & .354 & .750 & $<$.001 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-3B & .640 & 0.2 & 1.07 (0.81--1.22) & 1.70 & .630 & .642 & .601 & .570 & .823 & .916 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-7B & .768 & 0.7 & {\\bf 1.42} (1.07--1.46) & 1.79 & .770 & .759 & .739 & .710 & .860 & .392 \\\\\n        Qwen2.5-14B & {\\bf .841} & 0 & 1.15 (0.56--1.43) & {\\bf 1.82} & {\\bf .787} & {\\bf .793} & {\\bf .780} & {\\bf .750} & {\\bf .902} & .946 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & .341 & 0 & 0.44 (0.16--0.68) & 1.17 & .276 & .284 & .230 & .160 & .421 & .038 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & .549 & 0 & 0.72 (0.31--0.88) & 1.23 & .497 & .486 & .398 & .312 & .707 & $<$.001 \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & .628 & 0 & 0.69 (0.37--0.91) & 1.26 & .582 & .537 & .532 & .381 & .744 & .085 \\\\\n        Mistral-7B & .268 & 0.7 & 0.78 (0.14--1.05) & 1.39 & .264 & .238 & .241 & .191 & .421 & .946 \\\\\n        Gemma-2-9B & .610 & 0.4 & 1.34 (0.60--1.41) & 1.65 & .591 & .616 & .607 & .595 & .707 & .946 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{Code generation. Best $T$ maximizes \\passone. Larger models hold their \\passone far past $T = 0.8$, while the four significant drops from 0.6 to 1.0 are all at 3B or below. \\Tu CIs are wide because curves are flat near the threshold. Best value in each column is in \\textbf{bold}.}\n    \\label{tab:code}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/size.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Qwen 0.5B$\\to$14B $\\Delta$\\Tu} & \\textbf{Llama 1B$\\to$8B $\\Delta$\\Tu} & \\textbf{Spearman $\\rho$, 10 models ($p$)} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Stories & +0.47 (0.43, 0.55) & $-$0.05 ($-$0.09, 0.00) & 0.61 (.060) \\\\\n        QA & +0.87 (0.76, 1.11) & +0.27 (0.04, 0.48) & 0.90 ($<$.001) \\\\\n        Code & +0.57 (0.00, 0.93) & +0.25 ($-$0.15, 0.59) & 0.64 (.048) \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\caption{Size effects on \\Tu, with 95\\% CIs for the ladder contrasts. The Qwen ladder gains range on every task, though the code CI barely clears zero. The Llama ladder gains on QA only.}\n    \\label{tab:size}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/family.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Pair (7--9B, all AWQ)} & \\textbf{Shape gap} & \\textbf{$T$s with CI excluding 0} & \\textbf{Peak $T$} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Qwen 7B vs.\\ Llama 8B & {\\bf 0.220} & 9 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.0 \\\\\n        Llama 8B vs.\\ Mistral 7B & 0.205 & {\\bf 10} & 1.0 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        Llama 8B vs.\\ Gemma 9B & 0.118 & 8 & 1.0 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        Qwen 7B vs.\\ Gemma 9B & 0.104 & 8 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        Mistral 7B vs.\\ Gemma 9B & 0.098 & 9 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        Qwen 7B vs.\\ Mistral 7B & 0.046 & 2 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Floor: seed replicate & 0.031 & 0 & 1.2 vs.\\ 1.2 \\\\\n        Floor: bf16 vs.\\ AWQ & 0.026 & 1 & 1.4 vs.\\ 1.4 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\caption{Family differences in semantic diversity curves, out of the 10 temperatures above 0. Llama pairs with Qwen and Mistral sit far above both floors, and Qwen and Mistral sit close to them. The largest values are in \\textbf{bold}.}\n    \\label{tab:family}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > tables/failures.tex <<'E'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}ccccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Code}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{QA}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Stories}} \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-6} \\cmidrule(lr){7-8}\n        \\textbf{$T$} & Passes & Syntax errors & Truncated & Correct & Rambling & Truncated & Non-ASCII \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        0.7 & 52\\% & 2\\% & 2\\% & 49\\% & 3\\% & 10\\% & 0.1\\% \\\\\n        1.0 & 48\\% & 4\\% & 2\\% & 47\\% & 2\\% & 14\\% & 0.5\\% \\\\\n        1.2 & 42\\% & 17\\% & 9\\% & 43\\% & 4\\% & 47\\% & 1.7\\% \\\\\n        1.4 & 32\\% & 45\\% & 35\\% & 37\\% & 12\\% & 70\\% & 5.5\\% \\\\\n        1.6 & 21\\% & 68\\% & 61\\% & 29\\% & 28\\% & 87\\% & 9.0\\% \\\\\n        2.0 & 5\\% & 93\\% & 89\\% & 11\\% & 58\\% & 92\\% & 11.4\\% \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\caption{Failure modes pooled over the 10 main models. Rambling means a wrong QA answer of more than 8 words. Between $T = 1.0$ and $1.4$, models stop ending their outputs and code stops parsing.}\n    \\label{tab:failures}\n\\end{table}\nE\ncat > sections/results.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\Tabref{tab:verdicts} summarizes our verdicts, and \\figref{fig:quality} shows the main pattern: quality holds near each model's peak and then falls, at a point that differs by model and task. \\Figref{fig:curves} shows all raw curves. Quality falls most abruptly on stories and more gradually on QA.\n\n\\input{tables/verdicts}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/quality_normalized.pdf}\n    \\caption{Quality relative to each model's own peak, with darker lines for larger models in a family. The dashed line marks 90\\%, which defines \\Tu. On stories, most models go from near their peak to near zero within one or two grid steps, and the break ranges from $T \\approx 0.8$ to 1.3 across models.}\n    \\label{fig:quality}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/curves_all.png}\n    \\caption{All metrics by temperature for all models and controls, with bands showing bootstrap 95\\% CIs. Vertical dashed lines mark $T = 0.7$ and $1.2$. Dotted gray lines are the control runs, which closely follow the matching main runs in shape.}\n    \\label{fig:curves}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{H1: open-ended generation}\n\\label{sec:h1}\n\n{\\bf Does diversity plateau above 1.2?} In a sense it does, but ``plateau'' is the wrong shape. Semantic diversity rises, peaks at $T$ of 1.0--1.4, and then falls in 9 of 10 models (\\tabref{tab:stories}). Qwen2.5-14B is the exception, with no slowdown between the two ranges (ratio 0.98, 95\\% CI 0.69--1.30).\n\n\\input{tables/stories}\n\nMost of this pattern comes from the measures, not the models. Distinct-2 flattens because it reaches its ceiling of 1.0. We read the fall in semantic diversity as an embedding artifact: degenerate text appears to embed similarly whatever it says, so a falling score does not mean outputs are less varied. We did not test this reading directly. Restricting to prompts where both judged samples are coherent supports it, because semantic diversity then rises at almost every temperature until too few coherent groups remain. For example, Gemma rises from 0.18 at $T = 0.2$ to 0.37 at $T = 1.4$. This is a selected subset, but it suggests that coherence loss, not saturation, ends useful diversity (\\figref{fig:frontier}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/story_frontier.pdf}\n    \\caption{Diversity against coherence as $T$ rises, with hollow markers at $T = 1.2$. Coherence stays flat while diversity rises, then drops steeply. \\figleft: semantic diversity turns back on itself past the cliff. \\figright: distinct-2 presses against its ceiling.}\n    \\label{fig:frontier}\n\\end{figure}\n\n{\\bf Does coherence drop 20--40\\%?} This prediction is met only in passing. At $T = 1.4$ Qwen2.5-14B falls 19\\%, close to the range, while the other nine models lose 48--62\\%. These figures understate the damage: the judge rarely scores below 3, so the raw scale cannot fall more than about 64\\%. The coherent rate shows it better, falling from about 1.0 to about 0 within one or two grid steps. For example, Mistral goes from 0.995 at $T = 1.2$ to 0.13 at $T = 1.4$.\n\n\\subsection{H2: factual QA}\n\\label{sec:h2}\n\n{\\bf Does accuracy degrade above 0.7?} Yes, but the loss is small (\\tabref{tab:qa}). Accuracy at $T$ of 0.8--1.0 is below that at $T \\le 0.7$ in all 10 models and significantly so in 9, by 2.0--5.2 points. Qwen2.5-14B loses 0.7 points, which is not significant ($p = .217$). The decline stays gradual up to about $T = 1.2$ and steepens beyond it.\n\n{\\bf Does the extra diversity help?} Below 0.7 it does: \\coverfour at $T = 0.7$ is 2.7--9.7 points above greedy accuracy. Above 0.7 it does not. Moving from 0.7 to 1.0 changes coverage by $-$5.0 to +1.7 points, $-$0.8 on average, even though distinct answers keep rising. The extra diversity is mostly more wrong answers.\n\n\\subsection{H3: code generation}\n\\label{sec:h3}\n\n{\\bf Is there a sharp collapse just above 0.8?} No (\\tabref{tab:code}). No model drops significantly from 0.6 to 0.8 after Holm correction. From 0.6 to 1.0 four models drop significantly, all at 3B or below, and the largest relative drop is 28\\% (Qwen2.5-0.5B), under the 30\\% we set as our example of a sharp drop in the plan.\n\n{\\bf Is the useful range 0.2--0.6?} The lower bound is not supported: $\\Tlo = 0$ for every model, and five models reach their best \\passone at greedy. The upper bound varies from 0.44 to 1.42, and Qwen2.5-3B, -7B, -14B and Gemma all stay within 90\\% of peak to at least 1.07.\n\n{\\bf Where does temperature pay off?} It pays off when several attempts can be tested. At $T = 0.8$, \\passfour exceeds greedy \\passone by 6--18 points in every model, led by Qwen2.5-3B at +18.3. Across models, the best temperature for \\passfour ranges from 0.4 to 1.4, and for five models it is 0.7 or 0.8.\n\n{\\bf Where is the real collapse?} It comes later and is syntactic (\\tabref{tab:failures}). Pooled across models, syntax errors go from 2\\% at $T = 0.8$ to 17\\% at 1.2, 45\\% at 1.4, and 93\\% at 2.0.\n\n\\input{tables/code}\n\n\\subsection{H4: model size}\n\\label{sec:h4}\n\nLarger models tolerate higher temperature in the Qwen family on all three tasks, though the code contrast only just clears zero and is not monotonic, since Qwen2.5-14B has a lower \\Tu than Qwen2.5-7B (\\tabref{tab:size}, \\figref{fig:ranges}). In the Llama family the trend holds on QA only. Story \\Tu is 1.01 at 1B, 1.00 at 3B, and 0.96 at 8B. Code \\Tfifty, which is less sensitive than \\Tu, also rises with size: 1.09 to 1.82 for Qwen and 1.17 to 1.26 for Llama. Spearman values within a ladder rest on 3--5 points, so we rely on the bootstrap contrasts instead.\n\n\\input{tables/size}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/useful_ranges.pdf}\n    \\caption{Upper bound of the useful range for each model, ordered by size, with 95\\% CIs. Story \\Tu shows Llama holding near 1.0 at every size while Qwen climbs with size. Code CIs are wide, so single-model code comparisons are weak.}\n    \\label{fig:ranges}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{H5: family differences at matched scale}\n\\label{sec:h5}\n\nCurve shapes differ across families, but not evenly (\\tabref{tab:family}, \\figref{fig:family}). Llama differs from the other three families by 4--7 times the seed floor, and it peaks at $T = 1.0$ rather than 1.4. The 1--1.5B and 3B Qwen-versus-Llama pairs show the same result, with gaps of 0.225 and 0.224. Gemma sits between the groups, and Qwen and Mistral are close to each other.\n\n\\input{tables/family}\n\nOn stories, family matters more than size in this sample. Llama-8B breaks at $\\Tu = 0.96$, close to Llama-1B at 1.01, while the other 7--9B models hold to 1.17--1.22. The evidence is weaker on the other two tasks: the seed floors for QA accuracy (0.061) and \\passone (0.051) are larger, and the largest 7--9B gaps reach only 0.115 and 0.213.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/family_matched_diversity.png}\n    \\caption{Diversity curves for the four 7--9B AWQ models. On stories, Llama peaks and falls earlier than the others. On code, Llama and Mistral become diverse at low temperature, while Qwen and Gemma rise slowly, so the family order is not the same on each task.}\n    \\label{fig:family}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Controls and failure modes}\n\\label{sec:controls}\n\n\\para{Seed.} Two sampling-seed runs of Qwen2.5-0.5B differ at no temperature on any metric.\n\n\\para{Precision.} Comparing Qwen2.5-7B in bf16 and AWQ, curve shapes sit at the seed floor (semantic diversity gap 0.026). Levels do differ: greedy \\passone is .829 versus .768, and greedy QA accuracy is .573 versus .553. AWQ therefore does not explain the shape differences above, but absolute levels should not be compared across precisions.\n\n\\para{Failure modes.} Above the cliff, models stop ending their outputs, drift into other languages, and emit code tokens in prose (\\tabref{tab:failures}). Story truncation at the token limit goes from 14\\% at $T = 1.0$ to 70\\% at $T = 1.4$.\nE\ncat > sections/discussion.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\para{What do the cliffs mean?}\nTemperature's useful range is set by where each model breaks, and fixed thresholds such as 0.7, 0.8, or 1.2 describe no single model well. The abrupt shape would fit a feedback process in which one random token that breaks the text makes further random tokens more likely, but we did not test this mechanism. We also cannot say why families differ. Logit scale and vocabulary are candidates, and we measured neither.\n\n\\para{What should practitioners do?}\nBelow $T \\approx 0.8$, temperature was safe on nearly every model we tested, but it is a weak diversity control. The common 0.7 default is therefore safe rather than optimal. Single-attempt code and QA do best at low $T$. If several attempts can be checked, moderate $T$ helps: \\passfour and \\coverfour both gain over greedy. Above $T \\approx 1.0$, the same setting can give intact output on one model and word salad on another of the same size, so the range has to be checked per model. At $T = 1.2$, Mistral-7B has a coherent rate of 0.995 and Llama-3.1-8B has 0.07.\n\n\\para{Limitations.}\nSeveral limits bound these conclusions.\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item {\\bf One judge with a compressed scale.} The Qwen3-8B judge rarely scores below 3 and shares a lineage with the Qwen subjects. An audit of 16 boundary stories agreed with the threshold on 12 (75\\%), with the judge slightly conservative. The audit was done by the research agent rather than by human raters, covers one model, and is small.\n    \\item {\\bf Embedding diversity misleads on degenerate text} (\\secref{sec:h1}).\n    \\item {\\bf Confounds.} Each family has one checkpoint per size, so family cannot be separated from checkpoint or quantizer differences, and precision co-varies with size. The Llama ladder also crosses versions 3.2 and 3.1.\n    \\item {\\bf Mistral's low code score.} Its \\passone of .268 is far below what its QA score suggests. Restoring prompt imports raised it only to .280, and most failures are real assertion errors, but the cause is unexplained. This score should not be taken as representative of Mistral-7B.\n    \\item {\\bf Scope.} We test pure temperature only, short stories, one prompt per task, and 4 samples per cell. $T = 0$ uses one sample, one seed replicate on one model is a thin noise floor, and vLLM batch composition can change outputs slightly. Results may not apply to hosted frontier models.\n    \\item {\\bf Resolution and thresholds.} Grid steps of 0.2 above $T = 0.8$ limit how precisely we can place a cliff, and \\Tu depends on the 90\\% threshold. Story \\Tu CIs are narrow partly because of interpolation.\n    \\item {\\bf Multiple comparisons} are corrected within tests but not across all hypotheses, and several analysis choices came after seeing partial data (\\secref{sec:method}).\n    \\item {\\bf Contamination.} All three datasets are public and may be in training data. This matters little for curve shapes and more for absolute levels.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\para{Broader implications.}\nInterfaces that offer a single temperature slider imply that a value means the same thing on every model, and our results show it does not. Benchmarks that compare models at one fixed temperature above 1 may partly measure where each model's cliff falls. Diversity metrics also need care, because a metric that falls on broken text can make a failing model look stable or a high setting look unproductive.\nE\ncat > sections/conclusion.tex <<'E'\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe measured diversity and quality on one grid of 10 open-weight models, 11 temperatures, and three tasks. Quality stays nearly flat and then falls at a model-specific point, with story \\Tu ranging from 0.84 to 1.31. Code has no sharp collapse just above 0.8, factual accuracy loses only a few points above 0.7 with no coverage gain, and the useful range grows with size in Qwen but mostly not in Llama. The main takeaway is that there is no universal temperature: the useful range is a property of the model, not of the setting.\n\nFuture work should measure logit scale and top-token margin to test whether they predict the cliff location, add more checkpoints per family to separate family from checkpoint, and run hosted frontier models through the same grid. Sampling at steps of 0.05 around each cliff would test how abrupt it really is, and a diversity measure that does not collapse on degenerate text, together with human coherence ratings, would remove the two largest measurement limits.\nE\npdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex >/dev/null; bibtex main | tail -3; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex >/dev/null; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex | grep -E \"^!|Overfull|undefined|Output written\" | head -30; wc -w sections/abstract.tex; grep -n \"figleft\\|figright\" commands/*.tex | head; grep -c . main.log; grep -E \"LaTeX Warning\" main.log | head","description":"Write all LaTeX sources and compile the paper"}}}
